<section id="judgment" class="chapter"><div class="chapter-title"><span class="index">12</span><div><h2>The senior-developer review desk</h2><p class="deck">Tradeoffs are more useful than commandments.</p></div></div>
<article class="wisdom"><p class="eyebrow">Practitioner note / Nicholas Nethercote</p><h3>Optimize the bottleneck you can demonstrate.</h3><p>The Rust Performance Book emphasizes hot code, better algorithms and data structures, and measurements. Start with a release build and a representative workload. Change one thing, compare results, and retain the measurement that explains a surprising optimization. Reducing unnecessary work can matter more than making a tiny operation clever.</p><p class="application">Apply it: measure the starter on a large file before replacing its readable parser. If memory becomes the issue, investigate streaming I/O before low-level tricks.</p></article>
<div class="split"><div><h3>Questions worth asking in review</h3><ul><li>Who owns this data, and how long must it live?</li><li>Can this invalid state be constructed outside its module?</li><li>Does the caller have enough context to handle this error?</li><li>Are allocation, I/O and locking visible where they matter?</li><li>What does cancellation leave partially done?</li><li>Which measured requirement justifies this abstraction?</li></ul></div><aside><h3>Keep expert advice in context</h3><p>The named notes summarize each author's published reasoning. They are not quotes, a consensus vote, or evidence that one technique always wins. The examples and review questions are this primer's synthesis.</p><p>Unsafe, procedural macros, pinning internals and FFI belong to later study. Unsafe requires proving obligations that the compiler no longer checks; it does not switch off ordinary type checking.</p></aside></div>
